Don’t let yourself be unhappy because you don’t like your smile.

There’s a beautiful paradox in that.

You dislike the way you look when you express happiness, so you suppress the very happiness that produces it.

You sacrifice the experience because you dislike its appearance.

And that pattern extends far beyond smiling.

People avoid dancing because they think they look foolish. Avoid laughing because they dislike their laugh. Avoid loving because they fear rejection. Avoid trying because they fear looking incompetent.

They become spectators of their own lives, constantly evaluating themselves instead of experiencing them.

And here’s another layer:

Your smile isn’t only something you see. It’s something other people experience.

What you perceive as an imperfection might be precisely what someone who loves you finds beautiful.

The same applies to personality, laughter, enthusiasm, vulnerability, and countless other things.

There’s a distinction between living a life and watching yourself live it.

Self-awareness can improve your life. But excessive self-surveillance can imprison you inside your own judgment.

Don’t let the way you think happiness should look prevent you from actually being happy.

That’s worth passing down to your boys, too.

